WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.540
- [Narrator] This is
Betty, a blue collar worker

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.030
who tends the counter at
a fast food restaurant.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.450
And this is Wilma, she's
a white collar worker

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.100
who writes computer code.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.160
Both make average salaries
for those two groups.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.980
From 2010 to 2012, the salary

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.430
for Wilma's position would
have grown more than Betty's.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.220
But from 2020 to 2022,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.070
it was Betty that would
get the bigger bump.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.860
Wage gains for some blue
collar workers have started

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.260
to outpace gains for white collar workers

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.180
over the last three years,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.820
a reversal from a decades-long trend.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.610
- This is a pretty
remarkable moment in history.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.740
- [Narrator] Here's why wages
have been growing faster

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.360
for low-income workers,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.663
and why those gains have
helped stave off a recession.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.400
First, it's important to
understand who exactly qualifies

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.380
as blue and white collar.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.410
- Blue collar has become a
more all-encompassing term

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.000
for lots of different wage
workers, often at the lower end

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.220
of the income spectrum,
but not exclusively.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.300
And white collar tends to be
more salaried professionals

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.700
who are typically working
in office-like settings.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.920
- [Narrator] Non-management
blue collar jobs tended

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.380
to include people that
work in restaurants,

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.707
hotels, factories, retail
stores, and construction.

00:01:16.707 --> 00:01:21.450
White collar jobs include some,
but not all office workers,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.450
like people in finance, tech, and law.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.940
The pay gap between some industries

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.120
that fall in these groups
has steadily grown.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.273
You can especially see that
divide between Betty and Wilma.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.870
Part of the reason that
gap has grown in the past.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.110
- There has just been so
much demand for skills

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:44.050
like computer engineering,
like software engineering

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.520
and not as much supply.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.010
And there has been far more supply

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.320
of workers prepared to do low wage work.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.120
- [Narrator] The pandemic also increased

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.800
the need for white collar jobs,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.110
which could easily be worked from home.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.480
But when the economy began recovering,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.090
a different demand arose.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.730
- To what hurt us the most is,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.070
it's hard to find people to work.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.350
- [Narrator] People returned
to activities far faster

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.870
than companies could
hire, creating competition

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.660
for many traditionally low wage positions.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.290
And many blue collar workers
didn't return at all.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.790
- We had a lot of retirees

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.430
during the pandemic who have not returned

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.060
to the labor market, and
immigration was depressed as well.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.100
So we had fewer people
entering the country

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.250
to fill especially lower income jobs.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.960
- [Narrator] Many workers
were also quitting

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.390
their current jobs, which could be a sign

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.670
of more worker leverage.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.590
- When workers are
quitting in large numbers,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.120
it's because they're easily able

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.250
to exchange their jobs for better jobs.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.590
And when workers are being
laid off in large numbers,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.560
it's because employers hold the upper hand

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.160
and are easily able to exchange
workers for better workers.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.240
- [Narrator] Workers who did leave

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.630
for higher-paying jobs
also saw higher wage gains

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.220
than people who stayed.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.980
- When employers are short-staffed,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.960
the immediate problem at hand,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.750
the fastest way to solve that
problem is to hire someone.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.210
They're often prepared to
throw huge amounts of money

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.180
at hiring a new candidate,
signing bonuses,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.580
you need a new benefit in
order to come on, okay, sure.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.310
- [Narrator] So as demand
went up and supply went down

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.860
for blue collar workers,
the opposite happened

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.710
for white collar workers,
which kept their wage gains

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.050
at a lower level than
they typically would be.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:29.070
- To the extent we have seen
shakiness in the labor market,

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.510
it has been in some of these
white collar professions

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.770
like the tech industry.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.410
- [Narrator] While some
economists consider the slowdown

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.690
in white collar jobs more of a return

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.050
to pre pandemic levels, the
boost in blue collar pay

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.340
is keeping spending elevated,
even during inflation.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.620
Hourly earnings surpassed inflation

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.320
for the first time in two years in July,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.410
largely driven by gains
for low wage workers.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.360
- We have actually seen some parts

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.060
of the economy receive real wage increases

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.430
that have expanded their purchasing power

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.380
and allowed them to buy more,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.300
to move into better homes,
to invest in better schooling

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.470
for their kids, and to
improve their life conditions.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.700
- [Narrator] Some economists say

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.150
that this increased spending
power for lower income workers

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:21.150
and the tight labor market
have been major factors

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.710
in avoiding a recession.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.790
- So typically in recessions,
lower income workers,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.100
by virtue of the jobs that they work in

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.550
or the tenure that they
have at those jobs,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.120
tend to be some of the first to be let go,

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.190
or to not be hired in the first place.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.410
If those workers are making more money

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.850
and they're remaining employed,

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.390
they are propelling
economic growth forward

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.760
and they are keeping
other people in their jobs

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.770
and they are preventing a
recession from happening,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.340
which creates a virtuous
circle for everyone involved.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.510
- [Narrator] While these earning boosts

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.620
have helped close the gap

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.440
between blue and white
collar workers somewhat,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.770
the amount that Betty,
our food worker and Wilma,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.030
our computer programmer make
today is still very different.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.310
And as federal reserve officials continue

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:09.750
to tackle inflation,

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.780
they're taking an aim at
cooling the job market.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.900
- What we're talking about is
having wage increases still

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:16.980
at a very strong level,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.300
but at a level that's consistent

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.730
with 2% inflation over time.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.440
- [Narrator] Wages have started to cool

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.320
and the trend has
reversed in recent months.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.660
But some economists are optimistic

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.960
that the gains per low
income workers will continue.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.890
- I don't think we're going
to see these wage gains erode

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.710
because the labor market is still tight

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.720
in those low wage industries

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.060
- But wage gains for blue
collar workers would need

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.190
to keep outpacing those for white collar

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.203
to continue closing the gap.

00:05:46.203 --> 00:05:48.786
(quirky music)

